# RapidR Studio for the web: response headers for hosts that read this file
# (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages). docs/security-audit.md SEC-17.
#
# index.html carries its own Content-Security-Policy (a meta tag); the header
# adds what only a header can say: no other site may put Studio in a frame.
# No CORS: run.html, the program's sandboxed frame, gets the runtime from
# Studio by message, never by fetching it. No Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy:
# that frame's opaque origin loads the program's pictures from here.
#
# run.html: only Studio's own page may frame it. runtime/rapidr-webview.html,
# the frame an RWEBVIEW's Html runs in, can't say that (its frame-ancestors
# would have to match run.html, which is at an opaque origin, and no source
# can): it is always sandboxed instead, at an opaque origin whoever frames
# it, so what runs in it never reaches Studio's storage
# (docs/security/audit-2026-11-05-web.md SEC-34). Nothing limits its Html
# beyond what run.html's own sandbox already does.
#
# Permissions-Policy tools=(self): only Studio's own page may offer tools to
# the browser's AI agents (the WebMCP draft's policy feature); no frame of
# another origin may (crates/rapidr-runtime-web/src/agent_tools_web.rs).
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Permissions-Policy: tools=(self)
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
/
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
/index.html
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
/run.html
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
/runtime/rapidr-webview.html
  Content-Security-Policy: sandbox allow-scripts allow-forms allow-popups allow-modals allow-downloads
